ingle, could form chow with W4-W5 or W5-W7? But W4 and W7 availability: W4:1 left, W7:3 left. W5-W6-W7 possible if I draw W7. Unlikely immediate.  \n- W8 pair: good keeper for pair.  \n- T2: single, T-suit. Could form chow T1-T2-T3 or T2-T3-T4, but T4 is depleted (0 left), T3:4 left, T1:2 left. T1-T2-T3 possible if draw T1 or T3.  \n- B5 pair: good.  \n- B8: single, could pair or chow with B6-B7-B8 or B7-B8-B9. B6:3 left, B7:4 left, B9:2 left.  \n\nAlso, I have two pairs (W8 and B5). Could aim for \"Seven Pairs\" (24 points) if I get more pairs. But I have 6 tiles now, need 7 pairs (14 tiles), meaning I need 5 more pairs (10 tiles) but I only have 6 tiles in hand, so I would need to replace singles with pairs, which is long shot. Not advisable.  \n\nBetter to aim for standard 4 melds + pair. Currently have 2 melds (chows), need 1 more meld + pair. My two pairs (W8, B5) could be the pair. So I need one more meld from remaining tiles: W5, W6, T2, B8.  \n\nPotential melds:  \n- Pung: if draw third W8 or third B5, that's a meld. But I would then need a pair from somewhere else.  \n- Chow: possible sequences:  \n    - W5-W6-W7 (need W7, 3 left).  \n    - T2-T3-T4 (T4 gone, impossible).  \n    - T1-T2-T3 (need T1 or T3).  \n    - B6-B7-B8 (need B6 and B7) or B7-B8-B9 (need B7 and B9).  \n\nSo best tiles to keep are those with chow potential: W5 and W6 can work together if I get W7. B8 needs B6-B7 or B7-B9. T2 needs T1-T3.  \n\nAlso consider discard safety: look at opponents' discards. Previous player played many B3, B4, B2, T4, etc. Next player played W4, T1, T8, etc. Across played various. Seems no obvious dangerous tiles.  \n\nBut I should discard a tile with low pairing/chow potential and high safety.  \n\nCompare each valid action:  \n- Play T2: T2 is single, T-suit. T4 is dead (0 left), so T2's only chow is T1-T2-T3. T1:2 left, T3:4 left \u2013 decent chance. But discarding T2 removes T-suit from hand (my meld is T456 already, so maybe no need more T). Could be okay.  \n- Play W8: I have a pair of W8, discarding one breaks the pair. W8 is a simple tile, not dangerous. But keeping pair is good for pair possibility.  \n- Play B8: single, B-suit, could form chow with B6-B7 or B7-B9. B6:3, B7:4, B9:2 \u2013 decent. Discarding B8 removes B-suit except B5 pair.  \n- Play W6: single, can combine with W5 for chow if get W7. W7:3 left.  \n- Play W5: single, already used in meld? Actually my meld [W3,W4,W5] includes W5, but that doesn't preclude another W5 in hand. W5 could pair if draw another W5 (2 left).  \n- Play B5: I have B5 pair, discarding one breaks pair. B5 is simple, maybe safe.  \n\nI think best is to keep pairs (W8 and B5) as they can be pair or pung. Among singles: W5, W6, T2, B8, I should discard one with least potential.  \n\nW5 and W6 can work together for a W-suit chow (W5-W6-W7). That's promising because W7 has 3 left.  \nB8 needs two specific tiles for chow (B6 and B7, or B7 and B9).  \nT2 needs T1 and T3 (or T3-T4 but T4 gone).  \n\nSo B8 and T2 are weaker. Between them, B8 may be slightly better because B6 and B7 are more abundant (3 and 4 left) than T1 (2 left) and T3 (4 left). But T2's chow T1-T2-T3 requires two draws, while B8's chow requires two draws as well.  \n\nBut also consider that my existing melds are W and T suits, so maybe keeping B-suit could diversify for \"All Types\" (6 points) if I get honors later. But I have no honors now.  \n\nGiven I have two pairs, I might aim for a pung of W8 or B5 to complete a meld, and use the other pair as pair. So I need to draw a third W8 or third B5. Keeping both pairs maximizes chance. So discard a singleton that doesn't affect that.  \n\nAmong singletons, which is least useful? W5 and W6 have synergy. T2 is isolated. B8 is isolated. Discarding T2 or B8. Look at opponents' discards: T2 has been played by next player and across player recently? Next player played T2 in history? Actually history: next player played T2 (line: Play T2). Across played T2. So T2 seems safe. B8 has not been played much? Unshown B8:4 left. No recent B8 discards. But B8 is simple, likely safe.  \n\nI'll discard T2 as it seems safe and has lower immediate potential given T4 shortage.  \n\nReason: I have two pairs (W8 and B5) which are valuable for forming the final pair or pung. Keeping W5 and W6 together offers chow potential with W7. B8 is a simple tile with some chow chances, but T2's chow options are limited due to T4 depletion. Discarding T2 maintains flexibility and follows safety from opponents' discards.\n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
